function _sfc_render(_ctx, _cache, $props, $setup, $data, $options) {
  return openBlock(), createElementBlock("div", null, [..._cache[0] || (_cache[0] = [
    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_7-starting-lighting-and-ignition-batteries" tabindex="-1">7. Starting, lighting and ignition batteries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-starting-lighting-and-ignition-batteries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Starting, lighting and ignition batteries&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_7-1-battery-passport-necessity" tabindex="-1">7.1 Battery passport necessity <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-1-battery-passport-necessity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.1 Battery passport necessity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An SLI battery does <strong>not</strong> require a battery passport under Article 77(1), irrespective of capacity. From 18 February 2027, the Article 13(6)(b) QR-code route applies and must additionally provide access to the SLI-specific recycled-content information in Article 13(6)(c).</p><p>See Section 3.4, “Determine whether a battery passport is required”, and Section 13, “Labelling, QR codes and battery passports”.</p><h2 id="_7-2-product-and-sustainability-requirements" tabindex="-1">7.2 Product and sustainability requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-2-product-and-sustainability-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.2 Product and sustainability requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Effect on SLI batteries</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recycled content</td><td>Documentation and later minimum recovered-content shares apply where cobalt, lead, lithium or nickel is present within scope</td><td>Article 8(1)–(3)</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity label</td><td>Rechargeable SLI batteries must bear capacity information from the conditional date</td><td>Article 13(2), (7), (10)</td></tr><tr><td>QR information</td><td>Article 13(6)(b) information plus SLI-specific recycled-content information</td><td>Article 13(6)(b)–(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Waste collection</td><td>Free take-back and separate collection without an Article 61 percentage target</td><td>Article 61</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Annual reporting by category and chemistry</td><td>Article 75(2)–(3)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Common requirements—including restricted substances, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, economic-operator duties and market surveillance—are addressed in Sections 10 and 11.</p><p>Article 11 does not impose its removability and replaceability duties on SLI batteries; it addresses portable and LMT batteries. Where an SLI battery is incorporated in a vehicle or other product, the battery and host product remain distinct regulated objects. End-of-life vehicle and WEEE treatment facilities must hand resulting waste batteries to the responsible producer, producer responsibility organisation or selected waste management operator and keep records (Article 65).</p><h2 id="_7-3-recycled-content" tabindex="-1">7.3 Recycled content <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-3-recycled-content" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.3 Recycled content&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Application trigger</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recovered-content documentation</td><td>18 August 2028 or 24 months after the Article 8(1) delegated act enters into force, whichever is later</td><td>Article 8(1)</td></tr><tr><td>First minimum shares</td><td>18 August 2031</td><td>Article 8(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Increased minimum shares</td><td>18 August 2036</td><td>Article 8(3)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For SLI batteries containing the relevant materials within Article 8’s scope, records operate at battery-model, calendar-year and manufacturing-plant level. Article 8(2) sets minimum shares of 16% cobalt, 85% lead, 6% lithium and 6% nickel; Article 8(3) raises these to 26% cobalt, 85% lead, 12% lithium and 15% nickel. The values remain subject to lawful amendment.</p><p>Article 8(4) excludes batteries already marketed or put into service before preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing. Check the delegated methodology and document format before treating the conditional disclosure date as operative. See Section 10, “Product, sustainability and safety requirements”.</p><h2 id="_7-4-labelling-markings-and-qr-code-information" tabindex="-1">7.4 Labelling, markings and QR-code information <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-4-labelling-markings-and-qr-code-information" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.4 Labelling, markings and QR-code information&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In addition to common Article 13 requirements:</p><ul><li>a rechargeable SLI battery must bear capacity information from 18 August 2026 or 18 months after the Article 13(10) implementing act enters into force, whichever is later (Article 13(2));</li><li>from 18 February 2027, its QR code must provide access to applicable Article 13(1)–(5) information, the EU declaration of conformity, any applicable Article 52(3) due-diligence report, and Article 74(1)(a)–(f) waste information (Article 13(6)(b)); and</li><li>the QR code must also provide access to the amount of cobalt, lead, lithium or nickel recovered from waste and present in active materials, calculated under Article 8 (Article 13(6)(c)).</li></ul><p>Where the battery’s nature and size prevent printing or engraving on it, Article 13(7) requires the label or QR code on the packaging and accompanying documents. Because the Article 13(6)(c) QR duty begins before the base-text Article 8(1) disclosure date, the information required during that interval remains unresolved (OQ-REG-006).</p><h2 id="_7-5-due-diligence-producer-responsibility-and-end-of-life" tabindex="-1">7.5 Due diligence, producer responsibility and end of life <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-5-due-diligence-producer-responsibility-and-end-of-life" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.5 Due diligence, producer responsibility and end of life&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Battery due diligence is not determined by passport necessity. It applies subject to the threshold and exclusions in Article 47 and Articles 48–52. The current application date must be verified against Regulation (EU) 2025/1561 because the controlled base Regulation predates that amendment. See Section 14, “Battery due diligence”.</p><p>A producer must register in each Member State in which it first makes an SLI battery available and may not supply there unless registered (Article 55(1), (2) and (12)). Producers or appointed producer responsibility organisations must take back, free of charge and without requiring a new purchase, all waste SLI batteries of the category they first made available in that Member State, irrespective of nature, chemistry, condition, brand or origin. They must provide territory-wide arrangements, suitable infrastructure and delivery to permitted treatment facilities (Article 61(1)–(4)). Article 61 sets no percentage collection target for SLI batteries.</p><p>Distributors must take back waste SLI batteries of categories they offer or offered, subject to Article 62, including its distance-sale and delivery rules. Annual producer and waste-operator reporting applies by category and chemistry (Article 75(2)–(3)). See Section 15, “Producer registration and extended producer responsibility”, and Section 16, “Waste collection, treatment and recycling”.</p><p>After a listed circular operation, the operator placing the battery on the market or putting it into service is treated as the manufacturer (Articles 38(11) and 45). This does not create an Article 77 passport. Article 13(9) may, however, require new labelling or marking and QR-accessible status-change information. See Section 17, “Reuse, preparation for repurposing, repurposing and remanufacturing”.</p><h2 id="_7-6-gs1-implementation-for-sli-batteries" tabindex="-1">7.6 GS1 implementation for SLI batteries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-6-gs1-implementation-for-sli-batteries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.6 GS1 implementation for SLI batteries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Regulation requires identification and information-access functions but does not prescribe GS1 standards.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>How GS1 standards may support it</th><th>Granularity and limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identify the battery model</td><td>An implementation may use GTIN. GTIN identifies the battery model or trade item</td><td>It must identify the battery, not merely the host vehicle or machine. A GTIN alone is not an individual identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Associate Article 8 records</td><td>A GTIN, production year and manufacturing-plant GLN may associate model/year/plant records</td><td>A GLN does not replace legally required plant information or an authority-issued identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish a production batch</td><td>An implementation may use GTIN plus batch or lot number. This structure identifies a batch</td><td>This can support traceability but does not create a passport or satisfy technical-documentation duties by itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Provide web access</td><td>A GS1 Digital Link URI may connect the identified battery to controlled resources</td><td>Article 13(6)(b)–(c) information must remain complete, current and accurate.</td></tr><tr><td>Identify parties and locations</td><td>An implementation may use GLNs. GLNs identify manufacturers, distributors, collection points and treatment sites</td><td>A GLN does not replace a producer-registration number or other legally issued identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Identify logistic units</td><td>An implementation may use SSCC. SSCC identifies a logistic unit containing SLI batteries</td><td>It does not identify the contained batteries; their relationship must be recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>Share visibility events</td><td>An implementation may use EPCIS. EPCIS captures events concerning batteries, logistic units, parties and locations</td><td>EPCIS does not prove compliance or replace conformity assessment, verification, certification, audit or an authority decision.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Because SLI batteries do not require individual passports, granularity follows the supported function. Model/year/plant granularity is material for Article 8; batch identification may support production and recall controls; serialisation may be used where individual traceability is operationally needed. Where an SLI battery is incorporated in or supplied with a vehicle or machine, identify the battery and host product separately and maintain their relationship.</p><p>See Section 12, “Battery and grouping identification”; Section 13, “Labelling, QR codes and battery passports”; and Section 19, “Supply-chain traceability and information exchange”.</p><h2 id="_7-7-category-specific-example" tabindex="-1">7.7 Category-specific example <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-7-category-specific-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.7 Category-specific example&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For a rechargeable lead-acid SLI battery supplied for a vehicle, an implementation may use GS1 identification structures. GTIN identifies the battery trade item, GTIN plus batch or lot number distinguishes its production batch, and GLN identifies the manufacturing plant for association with Article 8 model/year/plant records. From 18 February 2027, a GS1 Digital Link URI in the QR code may provide access to the applicable Article 13(6)(b) and (c) resources.</p><p>The host vehicle and battery remain separately identified. The physical capacity, separate-collection, Pb and CE markings remain subject to their own provisions.</p><h2 id="_7-8-key-dates-and-triggers" tabindex="-1">7.8 Key dates and triggers <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-8-key-dates-and-triggers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.8 Key dates and triggers&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Date or trigger</th><th>SLI-battery requirement</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18 August 2025</td><td>Separate-collection marking and Chapter VIII producer-responsibility rules</td><td>Articles 13(4) and 96(2)(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2026</td><td>General label and rechargeable-SLI capacity label</td><td>Article 13(1)–(2), (10)</td></tr><tr><td>18 February 2027</td><td>QR code with SLI-specific recycled-content access</td><td>Article 13(6)(b)–(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2028</td><td>Recovered-content documentation</td><td>Article 8(1)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2031</td><td>First minimum recovered-content shares</td><td>Article 8(2)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2036</td><td>Increased minimum recovered-content shares</td><td>Article 8(3)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Cd/Pb marking transition requires legal confirmation (OQ-REG-002). For each “whichever is later” trigger, establish the relevant act’s entry into force before calculating the operative date.</p><h2 id="_7-9-limitations-and-open-questions" tabindex="-1">7.9 Limitations and open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-9-limitations-and-open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7.9 Limitations and open questions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>SLI classification depends on specific design purpose, not weight, capacity or chemistry alone. A battery used for auxiliary or backup power remains an SLI battery only where it is specifically designed for the Article 3(1), point (12) starting, lighting or ignition function.</p><p>Current-law checks remain necessary for the label act (OQ-REG-001), Cd/Pb transition (OQ-REG-002), QR treatment of an inapplicable due-diligence report (OQ-REG-003), SLI QR information before Article 8 disclosure applies (OQ-REG-006), circular-operation QR information (OQ-REG-007), due-diligence date (OQ-REG-009), reporting (OQ-REG-011), collection-method amendments (OQ-REG-013), GS1 Digital Link governance (OQ-GS1-003) and GDSN coverage (OQ-GS1-001). GS1 identifiers and EPCIS support interoperability but do not establish legal compliance.</p>', 35)
  ])]);
}
